            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies light as God's presence, truth, and Christ as the savior and revealer, with widely recognized secondary meanings including the written law, moral purity, and the visible righteous witness of believers before the world.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines light specifically and exclusively as the visible, lived example of obedience to the law (righteous actions others can see).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God's nature, Christ, divine revelation, the written Word, and believers' visible righteous conduct.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The law lived visibly as an example; obedient actions seen by others.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the primary divine, Christological, and ontological dimensions of light (God's nature, salvation, spiritual illumination), restricting the symbol entirely to human ethical conduct and visible obedience.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction regarding the verses cited; however, the book treats a derivative, behavioral application (the law and good works) as the sole definition, implicitly omitting the primary divine referents recognized by the consensus.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book selects the consensus' secondary themes of 'divine law' (Prov 6:23) and 'believers' visible witness' (Matt 5:16), synthesizing them into a specific behavioral definition. By doing so, it narrows the broader consensus to a specific subset of its accepted meanings.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The overwhelming majority of biblical references to light (especially in the Johannine writings) point to God, Christ, and salvation. Defining light purely as human obedience to the law ignores its dominant theological use as a symbol for divine revelation and presence.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
